---
import BlogLayout from "../../../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro";
import ArticleImage from "../../../../components/ui/ArticleImage.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../../../components/layout/Breadcrumbs.astro";
---

<BlogLayout
  title="How to Qualify for a Mortgage as a Consultant With Multiple 1099 Clients | Cliffco Mortgage"
  description="Consultants with income spread across multiple 1099 clients face a specific mortgage documentation challenge. Here's how lenders evaluate it and how to present it effectively."
  type="article"
>
  <Breadcrumbs trail={[
    { name: "Home", href: "/" },
    { name: "Blog", href: "/mortgage-guides/blog/" },
    { name: "Multiple 1099 Clients Mortgage", href: "/mortgage-guides/blog/multiple-clients-1099-income-mortgage-consultant/" },
  ]} />

  <article class="container-prose pt-12 pb-16">

    <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">How to Use Multiple-Client Consulting Income to Qualify for a Mortgage</h1>

    <p class="mt-4 text-lg font-medium text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">For the Consultant Whose Income Comes From More Than One Place</p>

    <ArticleImage />

    <p class="mt-8">If you work as a consultant and your income comes from several clients rather than a single employer or contract, how a lender evaluates your application depends on what you're applying for and how your income history is organized.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What Multiple-Client Income Looks Like to an Underwriter</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">A conventional underwriter reviewing a consultant's application isn't troubled by income from multiple clients in principle. What they're evaluating is whether that income is stable, documented, and likely to continue. Multiple clients can support that case more effectively than a single client, because income that isn't dependent on any one relationship is less vulnerable to a single loss. A consultant who earned consistent income across several clients over two years is demonstrating a more resilient income structure than one who relied entirely on a single contract.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Documentation Challenge With Multiple 1099 Sources</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">The complication with multiple-client income isn't the number of clients. It's the documentation burden of accounting for all of them clearly. Each client relationship that produced income needs to be traceable through your tax returns and, depending on the loan product, your bank statements or 1099 forms. Income that appears in your bank account but can't be traced to a documented source creates underwriting questions.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">For consultants who invoice clients, maintain separate business accounts, and file their taxes cleanly, this documentation is usually straightforward to assemble. For consultants who mix business and personal accounts, receive payments through multiple platforms, or have income sources that don't generate 1099s because individual payments fall below the reporting threshold, the picture can be harder to present clearly. The goal is to make your income history legible to someone reading your financial documents for the first time.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">How Loan Products Handle Multiple-Client Income Differently</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Under conventional guidelines, all self-employment income from Schedule C is combined and averaged regardless of how many clients contributed to it. The total net income figure is what matters.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">Under a 1099 loan, lenders typically want to see 1099 forms from your clients and will use the gross income shown across all of them to calculate qualifying income. Multiple 1099s from different clients are not a problem as long as they're consistent over the qualifying period.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">Under a bank statement loan, the source of individual deposits matters less than the consistency and total volume of the deposit history. A consultant who receives payments from multiple clients into a single business account will show a deposit history that reflects all of those relationships without requiring each one to be documented separately.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What to Do, In Order</h2>

    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Organize your income by client and by year before you apply.</strong> Know which clients generated what income in each of the last two years. This preparation makes the documentation process significantly faster and cleaner.</li>
      <li><strong>Consolidate business income into a single dedicated business account if you haven't already.</strong> Multiple accounts receiving fragmented payments from different clients creates a documentation puzzle. A single account with clean deposit history is easier to present and easier to underwrite.</li>
      <li><strong>Confirm that your total 1099 income is consistent year over year.</strong> If client composition changed but total income remained stable or grew, that tells a positive story. If total income dropped significantly in one year, understand how that affects your two-year average before you apply.</li>
      <li><strong>Identify whether any clients represent a disproportionate share of your income.</strong> If one client accounts for the majority of your earnings, a lender may ask about the stability of that relationship. Be prepared to address it.</li>
      <li><strong>Choose your loan product based on which income calculation method produces the strongest qualifying number for your specific history.</strong> For some consultants that's conventional, for others it's a 1099 loan or bank statement approach. Run the numbers under each framework before you decide.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="mt-10">Map Your Income Before Your Lender Does</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Cliffco's Self-Employed Income Calculator can help you see how your total consulting income calculates under different qualifying frameworks so you know which product and which presentation of your income history puts you in the strongest position before the application process starts.</p>

    <!-- Embedded lite Self-Employed Income Calculator -->
    <div class="mt-12 border border-[var(--color-accent)]/25 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
      <div class="bg-[var(--color-accent)]/8 px-6 py-5 border-b border-[var(--color-accent)]/15">
        <p class="eyebrow text-xs mb-1">Quick check</p>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">Compare your qualifying income.</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">See how conventional lenders vs. bank statement lenders view your income - side by side.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="p-6">
        <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Year 1 net income - tax return ($)</label>
            <input id="art-se-y1" type="number" value="80000" min="0" step="1000" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Year 2 net income - tax return ($)</label>
            <input id="art-se-y2" type="number" value="85000" min="0" step="1000" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Annual depreciation add-back ($)</label>
            <input id="art-se-dep" type="number" value="5000" min="0" step="500" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Other add-backs ($)</label>
            <input id="art-se-add" type="number" value="0" min="0" step="500" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">12-month bank statement deposits ($)</label>
            <input id="art-se-dep2" type="number" value="180000" min="0" step="1000" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Business expense ratio (%)</label>
            <input id="art-se-ratio" type="number" value="40" min="0" max="90" step="5" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
        </div>
        <button id="art-se-btn" class="btn btn-primary mt-5">Compare My Income</button>

        <div id="art-se-result" class="hidden mt-6">
          <div class="p-5 bg-[var(--color-mist-gray)] rounded-xl">
            <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] uppercase tracking-wide">Conventional Qualifying Income</p>
                <p id="art-se-conv" class="text-3xl font-bold text-[var(--color-ink)] mt-1"></p>
                <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">per month</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] uppercase tracking-wide">Bank Statement Qualifying Income</p>
                <p id="art-se-bank" class="text-3xl font-bold text-[var(--color-ink)] mt-1"></p>
                <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">per month</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] uppercase tracking-wide">Recommended Path</p>
                <p id="art-se-rec" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-1"></p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">Conventional path uses 24-month average (or lower year if declining). Bank statement path uses deposits minus expense ratio, annualized. Both are estimates - actual lender guidelines vary.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-4 p-5 bg-[var(--color-rich-black)] rounded-xl flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-center gap-4 justify-between">
            <div>
              <p class="text-white font-semibold">Not sure which path fits your income?</p>
              <p class="text-white/70 text-sm mt-1">A Cliffco self-employed specialist can run both scenarios with real lender guidelines and tell you what you qualify for.</p>
            </div>
            <a href="/get-started/" class="btn btn-primary !bg-white !text-[var(--color-rich-black)] shrink-0 whitespace-nowrap">Talk to a loan officer</a>
          </div>

          <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-center text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">
            Want to model other scenarios? <a href="/mortgage-guides/calculators/self-employed-income/" class="text-[var(--color-accent)] font-medium">View the full self-employed income calculator →</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </article>
</BlogLayout>

<style>
  .art-calc-input {
    width: 100%;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-soft-gray, #d1d5db);
    border-radius: 0.5rem;
    padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    background: white;
    outline: none;
    transition: border-color 0.15s;
  }
  .art-calc-input:focus {
    border-color: var(--color-accent);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 15%, transparent);
  }
</style>

<script>
  const seBtn = document.getElementById("art-se-btn")!;
  seBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
    const y1 = +(document.getElementById("art-se-y1") as HTMLInputElement).value;
    const y2 = +(document.getElementById("art-se-y2") as HTMLInputElement).value;
    const dep = +(document.getElementById("art-se-dep") as HTMLInputElement).value;
    const add = +(document.getElementById("art-se-add") as HTMLInputElement).value;
    const deposits = +(document.getElementById("art-se-dep2") as HTMLInputElement).value;
    const ratio = +(document.getElementById("art-se-ratio") as HTMLInputElement).value;

    const convAnnual = y2 < y1
      ? y2 + dep + add
      : ((y1 + y2) / 2) + dep + add;
    const convMonthly = convAnnual / 12;
    const bankMonthly = (deposits * (1 - ratio / 100)) / 12;
    const fmt = (v: number) => "$" + Math.round(v).toLocaleString("en-US");

    (document.getElementById("art-se-conv") as HTMLElement).textContent = fmt(convMonthly);
    (document.getElementById("art-se-bank") as HTMLElement).textContent = fmt(bankMonthly);

    const recEl = document.getElementById("art-se-rec") as HTMLElement;
    if (bankMonthly > convMonthly * 1.1) recEl.textContent = "Bank Statement";
    else if (convMonthly > bankMonthly * 1.1) recEl.textContent = "Conventional";
    else recEl.textContent = "Compare both";

    (document.getElementById("art-se-result") as HTMLElement).classList.remove("hidden");
  });
</script>
